Program Bug_2_80_ClassAssignmentSilentNoop;
// Bug 2.80: whole-class assignment (`b := a;`, both `class` variables, no
// field selector on either side) compiles cleanly with exit code 0 and
// produces a working .prg - no error, no warning - but doesn't actually
// copy any fields. Found while fixing/re-verifying bug 2.67 (whole-
// record assignment)'s own scope: 2.67's original catalogue entry
// claimed the same "Cannot assign a record..." compile error applied to
// both `record` and `class`, but that was never true -
// `ValidateAssignStatement`'s rejection (abstractcodegen.cpp) already
// required the right-hand side NOT be a class before it fired at all,
// confirmed against the unmodified pre-2.67-fix source directly.
//
// Root cause: every intentional record/class-assignment branch in
// AbstractCodeGen::AssignVariable (both ValidateAssignStatement's
// rejection and the later "RECORDS AND CLASSES DIRECT ASSIGNMENT" gate
// that routes into AssignPureRecords) explicitly excludes a `class` on
// either side. AssignPureRecords itself does contain a real dispatch to
// HandleNodeAssignCopyClass for a class, but that function's entire
// body is commented out ("NOT IMPLEMENTED, SEE PARSER FOR COPYING") and
// unreachable besides. With every intentional path excluding classes,
// execution falls through to the generic scalar assignment path, which
// treats `a`/`b` as ordinary single-value variables instead of doing a
// per-field copy.
//
// Deliberately not fixed yet - needs a real design decision (implement
// HandleNodeAssignCopyClass's commented-out body vs. make this a hard
// compile error, see KNOWN_BUGS.md), not a narrow one-line fix. Pre-fix,
// this snippet is expected to be COMPILE_OK (exit 0, .prg produced) but
// FAIL at runtime (the copy silently didn't happen) - the opposite shape
// from 2.67's own pre-fix COMPILE_ERROR.
@use "unittests/common"

var
	Point = class
		x, y : byte;
	end;

	a, b : Point;

begin
	disableciainterrupts();
	preventirq();
	setmemoryconfig(1,0,0);
	Common::TestInit();

	a.x := 10;
	a.y := 20;
	b.x := 0;
	b.y := 0;

	b := a;

	Vars::text := "2.80";
	if ((b.x = 10) and (b.y = 20)) then Common::PASS() else Common::FAIL();

	@addmonitorcommand "command 1  \"save \\\"$DIR/results.bin\\\" 0 8000 8000  ; quit \"  "
	addbreakpoint();
	moveto(5,24,04);
	if (peek(^$8000,0)=0) then
		printstring("PASS",0,40)
	else
		printstring("FAIL",0,40);
	loop();
end.
